  "text": "Solution 3 is correct under the standard no-minimum-loop model (Motzkin structures), where the answer is 1/2. I checked this by deriving the asymptotic covariance myself.\n\n**Setup.** Let x mark basepairs and y mark hairpins. Decomposing on the first base gives S = 1 + zS + xz²S[y/(1−z) + S − 1/(1−z)]. At x = y = 1 this is the Motzkin equation, with dominant singularity ρ = 1/3.\n\n**Singularity.** The singularity satisfies 1 − z − xz²(y−1)/(1−z) = 2√x·z. Write x = e^s and y = e^t, and let z(s,t) be the solution. Then K(s,t) = −log z(s,t) + log 3 is the per-n cumulant generating function.\n\n**Derivatives at (0,0).** Implicit differentiation gives:\n- z_s = −1/9, z_t = −1/18\n- z_ss = 1/54, z_tt = −1/108, z_st = 1/108\n\nSo K_s = 1/3 and K_t = 1/6, matching the means E[B_n] ~ n/3 and E[H_n] ~ n/6. The second derivatives are K_ss = 1/18, K_tt = 1/18, K_st = 1/36.\n\n**Correlation.** r = (1/36)/(1/18) = 1/2.\n\nSolution 1 uses the wrong decomposition and reaches 1/√2. Solution 2 uses an incorrect generating function and gets a negative correlation, which is implausible here. Both are wrong. If the problem instead intended a minimum hairpin size of 1 or 3, the answer would differ. My rough check for θ = 1 gives about 0.52, which is not a clean closed form, so 1/2 is the most plausible intended answer.\n\nCHOICE: 3",
